00:48:11.630 --> 00:48:12.909
ignacio: Yes, we got speed.

335
00:48:12.910 --> 00:48:14.019
Francesco Risitano (Tau): Yeah, okay, cool.

336
00:48:14.290 --> 00:48:16.499
Francesco Risitano (Tau): Yeah, so the…

337
00:48:20.010 --> 00:48:25.319
Francesco Risitano (Tau): The primary type that has been introduced is the execution-proof envelope.

338
00:48:25.630 --> 00:48:30.549
Francesco Risitano (Tau): And, this is essentially the type that's sent over the wire.

339
00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:41.870
Francesco Risitano (Tau): Proof data remains the same, proof type remains the same, but instead of keying based on the new payload request route, we key on the beacon block route.

340
00:48:41.910 --> 00:48:55.329
Francesco Risitano (Tau): And this just makes, kind of integration and association with the block far simpler, because it aligns with the data model that is, you know, extensively used throughout the rest of the client.

341
00:48:55.770 --> 00:49:02.180
Francesco Risitano (Tau): And then, secondly, on the P2P interface,

342
00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:07.859
Francesco Risitano (Tau): There's been a small change in regards to,

343
00:49:08.150 --> 00:49:14.579
Francesco Risitano (Tau): In regards to, how we verify payloads that are, execution proofs that have been received.

344
00:49:14.710 --> 00:49:24.720
Francesco Risitano (Tau): So… I guess… The main change here is,

345
00:49:24.980 --> 00:49:35.380
Francesco Risitano (Tau): that we actually do the proof engine verification inside of the, the gossip handler. And the reason that we do this is because,

346
00:49:36.090 --> 00:49:39.989
Francesco Risitano (Tau): Like, attribution of invalid proofs.

347
00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:45.510
Francesco Risitano (Tau): Needs to be more… needs to be stricter than

348
00:49:45.620 --> 00:49:54.260
Francesco Risitano (Tau): when you receive, say, a beacon block or, an execution payload. And the reason for that is, the number of…

349
00:49:54.310 --> 00:50:11.699
Francesco Risitano (Tau): entities or validators that can produce execution proofs is much larger, whereas typically for a single slot, there's one proposer and one builder. So, even if you do receive an invalid beacon block or execution payload.

350
00:50:11.770 --> 00:50:25.460
Francesco Risitano (Tau): and gossip it exclusively after verifying the signature, and not actually verifying the block, which is typically done. It's gossiped just after signature verification. The impact of that is pretty minimal.

351
00:50:25.750 --> 00:50:32.520
Francesco Risitano (Tau): But in the context of execution proofs, where you can have many different validators producing

352
00:50:32.650 --> 00:50:48.340
Francesco Risitano (Tau): proofs, many of which can be invalid. And if you're only, gating, like, gossip on the signature verification, then you can have spam on the network of

353
00:50:48.460 --> 00:50:54.199
Francesco Risitano (Tau): You know, lots of invalid proofs, essentially. So this is essentially a more conservative

354
00:50:54.360 --> 00:51:05.470
Francesco Risitano (Tau): approach. We still need to do some benchmarking because, this will have a tangible impact on, proof dissemination latency.

355
00:51:05.720 --> 00:51:25.009
Francesco Risitano (Tau): Because ultimately, you introduce around 80 milliseconds or so of verification latency. We still need to get some more extensive benchmarks there, but, yeah, ultimately, we're kind of moving in the direction of being more conservative, at least for optional proofs.

356
00:51:25.090 --> 00:51:32.590
Francesco Risitano (Tau): Obviously in the context of mandatory proofs, the, the number of entities that can produce a proof per

357
00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:34.090
Francesco Risitano (Tau): slot.

358
00:51:34.290 --> 00:51:41.309
Francesco Risitano (Tau): Reduces to 1. So, in that case, it, you know, we can kind of revert to only verifying the signature.

359
00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:42.780
Francesco Risitano (Tau): And then…

360
00:51:42.970 --> 00:51:51.849
Francesco Risitano (Tau): And then, and then gossiping immediately. So, in that context, you know, latency should be improved somewhat.

361
00:51:52.090 --> 00:52:02.420
Francesco Risitano (Tau): And I guess there's also work being done in regards to erasure-coded broadcast, of execution payloads, and,

362
00:52:02.820 --> 00:52:18.980
Francesco Risitano (Tau): I guess the implication is that if that dissemination strategy is successful and it reduces latency, then we would likely use the same strategy for execution proofs.

